Spring AOP doesn't modify your bytecode or inject code at compile time. It creates **runtime proxies** — wrapper objects that intercept method calls on the original bean. When you inject a bean that has aspects applied, you're actually injecting the proxy, not the original instance.

During context refresh, `BeanPostProcessor` implementations (specifically `AnnotationAwareAspectJAutoProxyCreator`) scan every bean after initialization. If any aspect's pointcut matches methods on the bean, Spring creates a proxy that wraps the original. The proxy intercepts method calls, runs the appropriate advice, and delegates to the original method.

This proxy-based approach has a fundamental limitation: **self-invocation bypasses AOP**. When a method inside the bean calls another method on the same bean using `this.method()`, the call goes directly to the original instance, not through the proxy. The advice doesn't fire.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) { /* transactional */ }

    public void batchPlace(List<Order> orders) {
        orders.forEach(this::placeOrder);  // Self-invocation — NO transaction!
    }
}
```

`batchPlace()` calls `placeOrder()` via `this`, bypassing the proxy. Each `placeOrder()` call runs without a transaction, even though it's annotated with `@Transactional`. This is the most common AOP-related bug in Spring applications, and understanding that AOP works through proxies — not bytecode modification — explains it immediately.

Spring AOP provides five advice types. `@Around` is the most powerful because it wraps the method call entirely, but using it when `@Before` would suffice adds unnecessary complexity and potential for bugs (forgetting to call `proceed()`).

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {
    @Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void logEntry(JoinPoint jp) {
        log.debug("Entering {}", jp.getSignature().getName());
    }

    @AfterReturning(pointcut = "execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))",
                    returning = "result")
    public void logSuccess(JoinPoint jp, Object result) {
        log.debug("{} returned {}", jp.getSignature().getName(), result);
    }

    @AfterThrowing(pointcut = "execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))",
                   throwing = "ex")
    public void logFailure(JoinPoint jp, Exception ex) {
        log.error("{} threw {}", jp.getSignature().getName(), ex.getMessage());
    }

    @After("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void logExit(JoinPoint jp) {
        log.debug("Exiting {}", jp.getSignature().getName());
    }
}
```

**`@Before`** runs before the method — use for validation, logging entry points, security checks. **`@AfterReturning`** runs only on successful return — use for audit logging or result transformation. **`@AfterThrowing`** runs only when an exception propagates — use for error monitoring. **`@After`** runs regardless of outcome (like `finally`) — use for cleanup. **`@Around`** wraps the entire call and controls whether the original method executes at all — use for timing, transactions, caching, circuit breakers.

The execution order when all five apply: `@Around` (pre-proceed) → `@Before` → method executes → `@AfterReturning` or `@AfterThrowing` → `@After` → `@Around` (post-proceed).

The classic `@Around` use case is method timing — measuring how long a method takes and reporting it. The implementation reveals two common mistakes: not handling exceptions, and not preserving the original exception type.

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class TimingAspect {
    private final MeterRegistry registry;

    public TimingAspect(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    @Around("@annotation(Timed)")
    public Object timeMethod(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
        Timer.Sample sample = Timer.start(registry);
        String methodName = pjp.getSignature().toShortString();
        try {
            Object result = pjp.proceed();
            sample.stop(Timer.builder("method.duration")
                .tag("method", methodName)
                .tag("outcome", "success")
                .register(registry));
            return result;
        } catch (Throwable t) {
            sample.stop(Timer.builder("method.duration")
                .tag("method", methodName)
                .tag("outcome", "error")
                .register(registry));
            throw t;  // Re-throw — don't swallow the exception
        }
    }
}
```

The `throws Throwable` declaration and the `throw t` in the catch block are essential. If you catch `Exception` instead of `Throwable`, checked exceptions from the target method get wrapped in `UndeclaredThrowableException`, confusing callers. If you don't re-throw, exceptions are silently swallowed and the caller receives `null`.

`proceed()` returns the target method's return value. Your advice must return it (or a transformed version) — returning `void` from an `@Around` method that wraps a non-void method returns `null` to the caller. The method signature should always be `Object` return type with `throws Throwable` to handle any target method signature transparently.

You can also call `proceed()` with modified arguments: `pjp.proceed(modifiedArgs)` passes different values to the target method. This is used for input sanitization, parameter transformation, and testing hooks.

The `execution()` designator matches based on method signatures. Its syntax looks complex but follows a fixed pattern:

`execution(modifiers? return-type declaring-type?.method-name(params) throws?)`

Each part accepts wildcards: `*` matches one segment, `..` matches zero or more. Let's break down the most common patterns.

```java
// All public methods in the service package
@Before("execution(public * com.example.service.*.*(..))")

// All methods returning void in any subpackage
@Before("execution(void com.example..*.*(..))")

// All methods named "find*" with any parameters
@Before("execution(* com.example..find*(..))")

// Methods taking exactly one String parameter
@Before("execution(* *..*(String))")

// Methods with no parameters
@Before("execution(* *..*())")
```

The `..` in `com.example..` matches any number of sub-packages. The `..` in `(..)` matches any number of parameters of any type. Without `..` for packages, only the exact package is matched — a common mistake when methods live in sub-packages.

A key performance note: avoid patterns that match too broadly. `execution(* *.*(..))` causes Spring to proxy **every bean** and intercept **every method call**. For a large application, this adds measurable latency to every operation. Scope your patterns to specific packages or use annotation-based pointcuts for precision.

The `@annotation()` designator is the safest approach for custom aspects because it's explicitly opt-in: only methods (or classes) carrying a specific annotation are matched.

```java
// Match any method annotated with @Timed
@Around("@annotation(com.example.annotation.Timed)")

// Match any method in a class annotated with @Audited
@Before("@within(com.example.annotation.Audited)")

// Bind the annotation value to an advice parameter
@Around("@annotation(timed)")
public Object time(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, Timed timed) {
    String metricName = timed.value();  // Access annotation attributes
}
```

When you use a **lowercase name** in the designator (`@annotation(timed)` instead of a fully-qualified class), Spring binds the annotation instance to a method parameter of that name. This gives you access to annotation attributes inside the advice — useful for configurable aspects where the annotation carries metadata like metric names or cache keys.

Combine pointcuts using `&&` (and), `||` (or), and `!` (not) for complex matching:

```java
@Pointcut("execution(* com.example.service..*.*(..))")
public void serviceLayer() {}

@Pointcut("@annotation(Transactional)")
public void transactional() {}

@Before("serviceLayer() && !transactional()")
public void logNonTransactional(JoinPoint jp) {
    // Fires on service methods that are NOT @Transactional
}
```

Extract reusable pointcuts as `@Pointcut`-annotated methods. This avoids duplicating the same expression across multiple advice methods and gives the pointcut a descriptive name. When the package structure changes, you update one `@Pointcut` method instead of hunting through every advice annotation.

The self-invocation problem is so common that every Spring developer needs to recognize it on sight. Here's the broken code and two production-grade fixes.

**Broken — self-invocation bypasses the proxy:**
```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Transactional
    public void processPayment(Payment payment) {
        chargeCustomer(payment);
        updateLedger(payment);
    }

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void updateLedger(Payment payment) {
        // Developer expects a NEW transaction here
        // But this runs in processPayment's transaction (self-invocation)
        ledgerRepository.save(new LedgerEntry(payment));
    }
}
```

`processPayment()` calls `updateLedger()` via `this`. The call never reaches the proxy, so `REQUIRES_NEW` is ignored. Both methods share the same transaction.

**Fix 1 — Extract to a separate bean:**
```java
@Service
public class LedgerService {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void updateLedger(Payment payment) {
        ledgerRepository.save(new LedgerEntry(payment));
    }
}
```

`PaymentService` injects `LedgerService` and calls `ledgerService.updateLedger()`. The call goes through `LedgerService`'s proxy, and `REQUIRES_NEW` works correctly.

**Fix 2 — Self-inject the proxy (less common):**
```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Autowired
    private PaymentService self;  // Injects the proxy, not 'this'

    @Transactional
    public void processPayment(Payment payment) {
        chargeCustomer(payment);
        self.updateLedger(payment);  // Goes through proxy
    }
}
```

Self-injection works because Spring injects the proxy instance. Calling `self.updateLedger()` goes through the proxy, activating `REQUIRES_NEW`. This approach is less clean than extracting a separate bean, but it's pragmatic when the methods are tightly coupled and extracting would create artificial separation.

The annotation should be self-documenting: reading it on a method should tell you exactly what behavior is being applied and how it's configured.

```java
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface Retry {
    int maxAttempts() default 3;
    long delayMs() default 1000;
    Class<? extends Exception>[] retryOn() default {RuntimeException.class};
}
```

Every attribute has a sensible default so that the common case requires no configuration: `@Retry` retries up to 3 times with 1-second delay on any `RuntimeException`. For specific scenarios, users customize: `@Retry(maxAttempts = 5, retryOn = {TimeoutException.class, IOException.class})`.

The `retryOn` attribute is critical for safety. Retrying on **every** exception — including `NullPointerException` or `IllegalArgumentException` — wastes time on bugs that will never succeed on retry. The default limits retries to runtime exceptions, and users narrow it further for production methods.

The aspect uses `@Around` with `@annotation()` pointcut to intercept annotated methods. Binding the annotation to a parameter gives the aspect access to the configured values.

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class RetryAspect {

    @Around("@annotation(retry)")
    public Object retryOnFailure(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, Retry retry) throws Throwable {
        int attempts = 0;
        Throwable lastException = null;

        while (attempts < retry.maxAttempts()) {
            try {
                return pjp.proceed();
            } catch (Throwable t) {
                if (!isRetryable(t, retry.retryOn())) {
                    throw t;
                }
                lastException = t;
                attempts++;
                if (attempts < retry.maxAttempts()) {
                    log.warn("Attempt {}/{} failed for {}, retrying in {}ms",
                        attempts, retry.maxAttempts(),
                        pjp.getSignature().toShortString(), retry.delayMs());
                    Thread.sleep(retry.delayMs());
                }
            }
        }
        throw lastException;
    }

    private boolean isRetryable(Throwable t, Class<? extends Exception>[] retryOn) {
        for (Class<? extends Exception> cls : retryOn) {
            if (cls.isInstance(t)) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

The `@annotation(retry)` binding — using the lowercase parameter name instead of the fully-qualified class — tells Spring to inject the annotation instance into the `retry` parameter. This is how the aspect reads `maxAttempts()`, `delayMs()`, and `retryOn()` from each annotated method's configuration.

The `isRetryable` check prevents retrying non-retryable exceptions. If the method throws `IllegalArgumentException` and only `IOException.class` is in `retryOn`, the exception propagates immediately without wasting retry attempts. After all attempts are exhausted, the **last** exception is thrown — not the first — because it represents the most recent state of the failure.

The most common proxy-related issues appear with Kotlin (final by default), Lombok's `@Value` (generates a final class), and libraries that check the concrete class type.

```java
@Service
public final class AuditService {  // final — CGLIB can't subclass
    @Transactional
    public void audit(AuditEvent event) {
        auditRepository.save(event);
    }
}
```

This fails at startup: Spring can't create a CGLIB proxy for a final class. The fix is removing `final` from the class (preferred) or extracting an interface and switching to JDK proxies.

The subtler case is final methods:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Transactional
    public final void processPayment(Payment p) {  // final method
        // CGLIB can't override this — @Transactional is SILENTLY ignored
        paymentRepo.save(p);
    }
}
```

No error at startup. No warning in logs. The method runs without a transaction because CGLIB can't override a final method. The data is saved without transactional guarantees, and you only discover the problem when a partial commit corrupts data.

For Kotlin, the `kotlin-spring` plugin automatically removes `final` from classes annotated with `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Configuration`, `@Transactional`, and other Spring annotations. Without this plugin, every Kotlin Spring bean would either need `open` on the class and every proxied method, or you'd need to switch all AOP to JDK proxies via `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(proxyTargetClass = false)` and extract interfaces for every bean.

Production-grade `@Async` requires three things: a bounded thread pool, an exception handler for void methods, and explicit context propagation for thread-local values you need.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig implements AsyncConfigurer {
    @Override
    public Executor getAsyncExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(5);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(20);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("async-");
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new CallerRunsPolicy());
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }

    @Override
    public AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler getAsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler() {
        return (throwable, method, params) ->
            log.error("Async method {} failed: {}", method.getName(),
                throwable.getMessage(), throwable);
    }
}
```

`CallerRunsPolicy` is a critical safety net: when the thread pool and queue are full, the task runs on the **caller's thread** instead of being rejected. This provides natural back-pressure — the caller slows down because it's doing async work itself.

For methods returning `CompletableFuture<T>`, exceptions are delivered through the future, not the uncaught handler. Callers must handle them:

```java
@Async
public CompletableFuture<Report> generateReportAsync(ReportRequest req) {
    Report report = reportService.generate(req);
    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(report);
}
```

To propagate `SecurityContext` to async threads, configure `SecurityContextHolder.setStrategyName(MODE_INHERITABLETHREADLOCAL)` — but be aware this only works for new thread creation, not thread pool reuse. For thread pools, use a `TaskDecorator` that captures and restores the context around each task execution.

Correct cache usage requires three annotations working together: `@Cacheable` for reads, `@CacheEvict` for invalidation, and `@CachePut` for updates that should refresh the cache.

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {
    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#id")
    public Product findById(String id) {
        log.info("Cache miss — loading from database");
        return productRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(id));
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
    public Product update(Product product) {
        return productRepository.save(product);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "products", allEntries = true)
    public void refreshAll() {
        // Clears entire cache — use after bulk imports
    }
}
```

The `key = "#id"` SpEL expression uses the method parameter as the cache key. Without an explicit key, Spring uses **all parameters** — which works for single-parameter methods but generates wrong keys for methods with parameters that shouldn't be part of the key (like a `Pageable`).

The `@CacheEvict` on `update()` ensures that the next `findById()` call gets fresh data from the database instead of a stale cached version. Forgetting cache eviction on write operations is the most common caching bug — the cache serves outdated data until the entry expires naturally.

For multi-JVM deployments, local caches (ConcurrentHashMap, Caffeine) diverge between instances. JVM A updates a product and evicts its local cache, but JVM B still serves the stale cached version. Distributed caches like Redis solve this, but add network latency to every cache operation. The choice depends on your consistency requirements: eventual consistency with local caches, or strong consistency with distributed caches at higher latency cost.

The retry-transaction combination is the most common ordering scenario. The correct order is: **retry on the outside, transaction on the inside**. Each retry attempt gets a fresh transaction, so a transient `DeadlockLoserDataAccessException` triggers a retry with a clean transactional state.

```java
@Aspect
@Component
@Order(1)  // Outermost — wraps everything, including the transaction
public class RetryAspect {
    @Around("@annotation(retry)")
    public Object retryOnFailure(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, Retry retry)
            throws Throwable {
        Throwable lastException = null;
        for (int i = 0; i < retry.maxAttempts(); i++) {
            try {
                return pjp.proceed();  // Each proceed() enters the @Transactional proxy
            } catch (Exception e) {
                lastException = e;
                if (i < retry.maxAttempts() - 1) {
                    Thread.sleep(retry.delayMs());
                }
            }
        }
        throw lastException;
    }
}
```

`@Transactional` has a default order of `Ordered.LOWEST_PRECEDENCE` (highest number = innermost). The retry aspect at `@Order(1)` wraps the transaction: retry → transaction → method. Each retry calls `proceed()`, which enters the `@Transactional` proxy, which starts a fresh transaction.

If the order were reversed (transaction outside, retry inside), all retry attempts would share one transaction. After the first failure, the transaction might be marked rollback-only, and subsequent retry attempts would fail immediately — not because of a transient error, but because the transaction is already doomed.

For custom `@Transactional` ordering, you can set it via `@EnableTransactionManagement(order = 100)`. This is useful when you need a custom aspect to run between retry and transaction — for example, a tenant-context-setup aspect at `@Order(50)` that configures the database schema before the transaction starts.

The self-invocation limitation is the most frequently cited reason for switching to AspectJ. Here's what happens with each approach.

**With Spring AOP (proxy-based):**
```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validateAndSave(order);  // Self-invocation — bypasses proxy
    }

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void validateAndSave(Order order) {
        // REQUIRES_NEW is ignored — runs in processOrder's transaction
        orderRepository.save(order);
    }
}
```

`processOrder()` calls `validateAndSave()` via `this`, bypassing the proxy. The `REQUIRES_NEW` propagation is ignored. Both methods share one transaction.

**With AspectJ (load-time weaving):**
```java
// Same code — but with AspectJ LTW enabled
// validateAndSave() gets its own transaction because
// the @Transactional advice is woven INTO the bytecode,
// not applied via an external proxy
```

With AspectJ, the transactional behavior is woven directly into `validateAndSave()`'s bytecode. Self-invocation works because the advice is part of the method itself, not an external wrapper.

To enable AspectJ load-time weaving, add `spring-aspects` to your dependencies and create `META-INF/aop.xml`. For compile-time weaving, configure the AspectJ Maven plugin. Both approaches add build complexity and can cause surprising behavior when woven aspects interact with Spring's proxy-based features.

The pragmatic alternative to AspectJ for self-invocation is refactoring: extract the method into a separate bean. This is simpler, more explicit, and doesn't require changing the AOP mechanism for the entire application.
